{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:cfdb01b4f19570efb79deacadb67cae5c0fd25ff133b189ba0a8ac219547d891","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.2},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.25},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.25}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"THE SHARED ASSUMPTION I AM BREAKING. The three Wave-1 P50s are 1.90, 1.90, 1.85 — a 5 bp spread across three supposedly orthogonal lenses. That is not independent estimation; it is anchoring on 2A's framing. Their shared, unexamined identity is: a capex bust (AI-Q4 at 66%) and a weak measured growth/TFP read are the SAME state of the world. The historical record of capex supercycles says the opposite, and P1 cited the record then drew the inverse inference from it: UK/US railways 1840s-1870s, US electrification, 1930s, telecom/fibre 1999-2002, shale 2014-2020 — in every case the capital-raisers were >=20% impaired AND the productivity arrived, because impairment transfers the asset to operators at a written-down basis and COLLAPSES the user cost of the service. P1 priced exactly this in his own asset matrix (power/grid 8.0% versus AI complex 1.0%) and then refused to carry it into his growth number. Fibre is the clean case: WorldCom/Global Crossing equity went to zero while 2000-2004 US measured TFP ran its fastest since the 1960s. Wave 1's CQ3 is therefore mis-signed on its own evidence.\nFOUR SPECIFIC BIASES I CHARGE. (1) Self-refuting cap magnitude: all three invoked Baumol/Jones weakest-link to kill the right tail, but P2 computed the reweighting drag himself at -0.12 pp/yr. A -0.12 pp mechanism cannot support a ~110 bp downgrade from the reference class. The cap binds on the LEVEL of bottleneck-sector output, not on the aggregate growth rate, when the bottleneck is 19-23% of GVA and the automatable slice is ~45%: with the bottleneck at exactly zero TFP growth, a 45% slice accelerating from 0.8% to 3.0% still delivers ~+0.95 pp to the aggregate. (2) Threshold-to-mean conversion error (scope insensitivity): P3 took AI-Q1 at 23% and booked \"~+0.25 pp, partly capitalized rather than productive.\" E[TFP lift] is not P(>=0.50 pp) x 0.50 pp; a distribution with 23% mass above 0.50 has a mean near 0.30-0.35, and capital deepening sits ON TOP of TFP, not inside it. (3) Drag double-count: the locked -2.7 pp/yr China and -1.1 pp/yr global figures are drags against a NO-AGING counterfactual, not level forecasts. China's working-age peak was 2011-2015 and measured growth already fell from ~10% to ~5%, i.e. most of that drag is in the run rate. P1 called \"-0.86 pp on the block\" the largest single term in his answer and P3 called \"-0.95 pp\" essentially the whole global figure, while both separately set China at 2.85-3.00% — the drag is being used twice, once as a rationale for the deceleration and once as a term. (4) Resolution-basis inconsistency: P3's own lens established that resolution uses the NBS official print, that NBS carries a documented +1-1.5 pp deflator/smoothing bias, and that official prints have never gone below ~2.2% ex-2020 — and then he centred the official print at 2.90%. Those three facts imply a higher centre, not a lower one.\nMY FERMI. PPP weights, constant 2025 USD PPP: China ~$42T, OECD-38 ~$76T, block ~$118T, so China 0.35 / OECD 0.65 (I reject P2's 0.36/0.64 and P1's 0.32/0.68 drift — the lock freezes 2025 weights, so there is no drift).\nOECD-38 2032-36: locked baseline 0.6%/yr per capita absent productivity offsets + 0.25%/yr population (OECD-38 contains Turkey, Mexico, Israel, US, Canada, Australia) = 0.85% baseline. Plus expected TFP lift +0.35 (mean of a distribution with 23% above 0.50, marked up for the aging demand pull that all three panelists conceded when they raised AI-Q2). Plus AI/grid/robot capital deepening at capital share ~0.33 on ~1% of GDP incremental investment: +0.12. Plus measured-GDP support, which is P3's own point used at full strength: hedonic GPU/software deflators convert nominal capex into large real volumes, and an impairment is a balance-sheet revaluation, so measured output never gives the capex back: +0.10. Plus a healthy-aging participation channel NONE of the three booked: 2A's \"70 in 2022 = 53 in 2000\" (41 countries, N~1M) is a labour-INPUT fact, and OECD 55-64 participation has already risen ~15 pp since 2000: +0.10. Minus Baumol reweighting at P2's own number: -0.12. Minus care/trades quantity rationing not already inside the 0.6% baseline: -0.05. OECD-38 = 1.35%/yr (P10 0.6, P90 2.1).\nChina 2032-36, OFFICIAL PRINT (the resolving object): 3.8%/yr (P10 1.8, P90 5.0). Justified by the NBS floor behaviour and smoothing bias, the target mechanism, the fact that the aging drag is largely realized, and the one place on earth where automation genuinely substitutes for missing workers at scale — China installs a majority of world industrial robots and faces the sharpest labour-scarcity demand pull in 2A.\nBLOCK = 0.65*1.35 + 0.35*3.80 = 0.878 + 1.330 = 2.21 -> P50 2.25.\nSPREAD: OECD sigma 0.55, China sigma 1.20, weighted contributions 0.36 and 0.42, rho +0.45 -> block sigma = sqrt(0.129+0.176+0.136) = 0.66. Symmetric band 1.40/3.10. I fatten the left tail to 1.20 for the joint capex-bust-plus-China-property/local-government-fiscal event (AI-Q4 at 66% is real and I am not disputing it; I am disputing what it implies for output), and set P90 3.25 (OECD 1.8% with a 4.5% official China print). Final 1.2 / 2.25 / 3.25.\nSTRONGEST WORLD IN WHICH THE WAVE-1 MODAL IS SIMPLY WRONG, AND ITS PROBABILITY: impairment happens on schedule (equity holders of the 2026-2029 commitment stack lose 40-60%), the written-down compute and the grid behind it pass to operators at a collapsed user cost, diffusion into the labour-scarce non-tradable segment accelerates on exactly the wage/automation-cost elasticity the panel conceded, China's official print holds 3.5-4.5%, the all-38 core aggregate prints above 2.7%, and the block clears 2.6%. P(growth >=2.5% conditional on an AI-Q4 impairment event having occurred) = 0.36. That is the specific proposition Wave 1's framing treats as near-contradictory, and it is the modal history of capital supercycles.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.616Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"China NBS annual real GDP print of <=3.0% for any single calendar year, or an explicit abandonment of a ~5%-type growth target in the official Five-Year-Plan framing.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly kills the single largest term separating me from Wave 1. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.25 to ~1.90 (-35 bp, i.e. all the way to the Wave-1 centre); CQ6-A falls from 33 to ~21 with the mass going to B (+8) and C (+4); my asset matrix's China-equity and industrial-metals buildout numbers drop 2-3 pp each.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.50% over any rolling 12 months with no active central-bank asset-purchase programme in place.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the monotone debt-supply ratchet that is the spine of my CQ4 and, through the basis-point construction of MOVE, of my CQ5. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.50 to ~1.85 and the skew flattens; CQ5 falls from 40 to ~27 (conjunct A back to ~0.42); long nominal sovereigns' regime-conditional numbers all shift up ~1.5 pp; regime D probability roughly doubles from 5 to ~10.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Turkey and Mexico core inflation both converge below 4.0%/yr on a 12-month basis, OR the OECD publishes and analysts adopt an 'OECD advanced' / ex-high-inflation core aggregate as the standard reference series.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the index-construction argument carrying CQ2 and the C/D split. CQ2 falls from 36 to ~24; CQ6-C falls from 28 to ~15, D rises from 5 to ~11, B rises to ~41; gold's regime-C number drops ~3 pp and long nominals' regime-C number improves ~2 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment above 6.5% for four consecutive quarters, with cognitive/information-sector (ISIC J, K, M) separations leading the rise.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms the BIS Box C labour-income demand shortfall as the operative channel rather than my labour-scarcity/demand-pull reading. CQ1 falls from 20 to ~9; CQ3 P50 falls ~30 bp to ~1.95 with the left tail to ~0.9; CQ6-D rises from 5 to ~14 and A falls to ~22; AI-complex and private-credit regime-D numbers are the live case rather than the tail.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE 5-year trailing average below 85 while the US 10y nominal Treasury yield averages above 4.25% over the same period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Breaks the level-dependence-plus-levered-intermediation mechanism that I used to push CQ5 conjunct A to 0.58 against Wave 1's 0.42-0.45. CQ5 falls from 40 to ~25; it also weakens the +20 bp term-premium arrow in CQ4, taking P50 to ~2.35.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 redteam seat, effort 'growth'."}}